CertHub.addPlanDetail("aws-developer", {"study":{"1":[["When would you choose SQS, SNS, EventBridge or Kinesis Data Streams?","SQS: buffer work for one consumer group, with retries and DLQs. SNS: push each message to many subscribers (often SQS queues for fan-out). EventBridge: route events by content pattern to many AWS targets, plus schedules. Kinesis: ordered, replayable streams read by several consumers."],["Explain how Lambda handles failures for synchronous, asynchronous and event source mapping invocations.","Synchronous: the error returns to the caller, who decides whether to retry. Asynchronous: Lambda retries twice, then sends the event to an on-failure destination or DLQ if configured. Event source mappings (SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams): Lambda retries the batch; use partial batch responses, bisecting, max retry age and on-failure destinations to avoid blocking."],["Calculate RCUs for 25 eventually consistent reads per second of 9 KB items.","9 KB rounds up to 12 KB, which is 3 RCUs for a strongly consistent read. Eventually consistent reads cost half, so 1.5 RCUs each. 25 × 1.5 = 37.5, rounded up to 38 RCUs."],["Compare local and global secondary indexes.","LSI: same partition key, different sort key, created only with the table, shares the table's capacity, supports strong consistency, 10 GB per partition key value limit. GSI: any partition and sort key, can be added any time, has its own capacity, eventually consistent only."],["Why initialize SDK clients outside the Lambda handler, and what is the catch?","The init code runs once per execution environment, so warm invocations reuse clients and connections and run faster. The catch: anything stored there is shared across invocations in that environment, so do not keep per-request data in globals."],["Contrast lazy loading and write-through caching.","Lazy loading fills the cache on a miss, so only requested data is cached but it can be stale and the first read is slow. Write-through updates the cache on every write, so data is fresh but the cache fills with items that may never be read. A TTL is commonly combined with both."]],"2":[["What is the difference between a Cognito user pool and an identity pool?","A user pool is a user directory that handles sign-up and sign-in and issues JWTs (ID, access, refresh). An identity pool exchanges those (or other IdP) identities for temporary AWS credentials tied to IAM roles."],["Describe envelope encryption with KMS step by step.","Call GenerateDataKey to get a plaintext data key and the same key encrypted under a KMS key. Encrypt the data locally with the plaintext key, discard it from memory, and store the encrypted data key with the ciphertext. To decrypt, call KMS Decrypt on the data key, then decrypt the data locally."],["When should you use Secrets Manager instead of Parameter Store?","Use Secrets Manager when you need built-in automatic rotation (for example RDS credentials), cross-account secret sharing or secret versioning labels, and accept the per-secret monthly charge. Use Parameter Store for configuration and non-rotating secrets at low or no cost."],["Execution role vs resource-based policy on a Lambda function: what does each control?","The execution role controls what the function can call (DynamoDB, S3 and so on). The resource-based policy controls who can invoke the function, such as S3, SNS, API Gateway or another account."],["List the API Gateway authorization options and when to use each.","IAM (SigV4): AWS principals such as other services or roles. Cognito user pool authorizer: users of a Cognito user pool with no custom code. Lambda authorizer: custom tokens or request parameters. API keys with usage plans are for metering and throttling clients, not for authorization."]],"3":[["Compare the Elastic Beanstalk deployment policies.","All at once: fastest, causes downtime. Rolling: batches, reduced capacity. Rolling with additional batch: keeps full capacity by adding a batch first. Immutable: new instances in a temporary ASG, safest and quickest rollback. Traffic splitting: canary-style testing with a percentage of traffic. Blue/green: separate environment and a URL swap."],["How do Lambda versions and aliases support safe deployments?","A version is an immutable snapshot of code and configuration; $LATEST is mutable. An alias is a named pointer to a version that can split traffic between two versions, which CodeDeploy uses for canary or linear shifting with alarm-based rollback."],["Walk through the AWS SAM workflow from a new project to deployment.","sam init creates a project, sam build packages code and dependencies, sam local invoke or start-api tests locally in Docker, sam deploy --guided creates the stack and saves settings, sam logs and sam sync help iterate, and sam delete removes the stack."],["Name the CodeDeploy lifecycle hooks for an EC2 in-place deployment in order.","ApplicationStop, (DownloadBundle), BeforeInstall, (Install), AfterInstall, ApplicationStart, ValidateService. The hooks in parentheses are run by the agent and cannot run your scripts."],["What are the phases of a buildspec.yml, and what else does it define?","install, pre_build, build and post_build, each with commands. It also defines env variables (including Parameter Store and Secrets Manager references), artifacts to output, reports for test results and cache paths."]],"4":[["What is the difference between X-Ray annotations and metadata?","Annotations are indexed key-value pairs (string, number, Boolean) you can search with filter expressions. Metadata can hold any object, including large ones, but is not indexed, so you cannot filter traces on it."],["A Lambda function behind API Gateway returns 502 or 504 errors. How do you tell the causes apart?","502 usually means Lambda returned an invalid proxy response or crashed. 504 means the integration timed out (29 seconds by default for REST APIs). Check the function logs for errors and duration, and fix the response shape or move long work to an asynchronous pattern."],["How do memory settings affect Lambda performance and cost?","CPU and network capacity scale with memory. More memory can shorten CPU-bound runs so much that cost stays flat or drops, because billing is memory × duration. Measure with tools like Lambda Power Tuning."],["What causes DynamoDB throttling when total capacity looks fine, and how do you fix it?","A hot partition: too much traffic on one partition key value. Fix it with a higher-cardinality key, write sharding (random or calculated suffixes), caching reads with DAX, or on-demand capacity for spiky traffic."],["How do reserved concurrency and provisioned concurrency differ?","Reserved concurrency sets aside, and caps, a number of concurrent executions for one function at no extra cost. Provisioned concurrency keeps a number of environments initialized to avoid cold starts and is billed while configured."]]},"domains":[{"id":1,"name":"Development with AWS Services","w":32,"topics":["Architectural patterns: event-driven, microservices, fan-out, choreography vs orchestration, loosely coupled and stateless designs","Resilient code: retries with exponential backoff and jitter, idempotency, timeouts, handling partial failures and dead-letter queues","Messaging and streaming: SQS standard vs FIFO (message groups, deduplication, visibility timeout, long polling), SNS fan-out, EventBridge rules and Scheduler, Kinesis Data Streams","AWS Step Functions: Standard vs Express workflows, retry/catch, task tokens for callbacks","Calling AWS services with the SDKs and CLI: credential provider chain, pagination, waiters, error handling","Lambda configuration: memory, timeout (15-minute max), ephemeral /tmp storage, environment variables, layers, concurrency, VPC access","Lambda invocation models: synchronous, asynchronous (retries, destinations, DLQs) and event source mappings (SQS, Kinesis, DynamoDB Streams, partial batch responses)","Lambda coding practices: initializing SDK clients and connections outside the handler, reading events, returning API Gateway proxy responses","DynamoDB: partition and sort keys, Query vs Scan, LSI vs GSI, RCU/WCU math, consistency models, condition expressions, TTL, Streams, DAX","Amazon S3 from code: multipart upload, storage classes and lifecycle, event notifications","Caching strategies with ElastiCache: lazy loading, write-through, TTLs; choosing between SQL, NoSQL and in-memory stores"],"notes":["Task statements 1.1–1.3 (DVA-C02 exam guide)"],"labs":["Free Tier: write a Python or Node.js Lambda function triggered by an SQS queue that returns batchItemFailures for bad messages, attach a dead-letter queue with maxReceiveCount 3, and watch a poison message land there. Lambda and SQS stay free at this scale; delete the queue and function when done.","Free Tier: create an on-demand DynamoDB table (customerId + orderDate), load 20 items with the SDK, then compare a Query with a Scan plus filter and note ConsumedCapacity for each. Add a GSI and a TTL attribute. Keep data tiny and delete the table afterwards.","Free Tier: build an SNS topic with two SQS queue subscriptions and an EventBridge Scheduler rule that invokes a Lambda function every 5 minutes; confirm both queues get every message, then disable the schedule so it stops running."]},{"id":2,"name":"Security","w":26,"topics":["IAM for applications: execution roles, instance profiles, ECS task roles, least-privilege policies, policy evaluation (explicit deny wins)","Resource-based policies: Lambda permissions for S3/SNS/API Gateway, S3 bucket policies, KMS key policies","Cross-account access with STS AssumeRole and trust policies; temporary credentials","Amazon Cognito: user pools (sign-up, sign-in, ID/access/refresh tokens) vs identity pools (temporary AWS credentials)","API Gateway authorization: IAM (SigV4), Cognito user pool authorizers, Lambda authorizers, API keys and usage plans","Encryption at rest: SSE-S3, SSE-KMS, SSE-C, S3 Bucket Keys, client-side encryption with the AWS Encryption SDK","AWS KMS: customer managed vs AWS managed keys, envelope encryption with GenerateDataKey, 4 KB Encrypt limit, cross-account key use","Encryption in transit: TLS, ACM certificates (us-east-1 for CloudFront), enforcing aws:SecureTransport","Secrets and configuration: Secrets Manager (rotation) vs Systems Manager Parameter Store (SecureString, tiers)","Keeping sensitive data out of code and logs: default credential chain, no hardcoded keys, CloudWatch Logs data protection masking","Presigned URLs for temporary S3 access; IAM Access Analyzer for least privilege"],"notes":["Task statements 2.1–2.3 (DVA-C02 exam guide)"],"labs":["Free Tier: create a Cognito user pool with a test user and an API Gateway HTTP or REST API with a Cognito authorizer in front of a Lambda function; call it with and without the ID/access token and compare the responses. Delete the API and pool afterwards.","Free Tier: write a script that generates a presigned PUT URL for a private S3 bucket, upload a file with curl, and then add a bucket policy that denies aws:SecureTransport=false and confirm plain HTTP is rejected.","Low cost: create one KMS customer managed key and run GenerateDataKey to encrypt a local file (envelope encryption), then decrypt the stored data key. Watch costs: a customer managed key costs about $1/month, so schedule it for deletion (7-day minimum) when finished. Store a test value in Parameter Store as a SecureString (standard tier is free) instead of Secrets Manager, which bills per secret."]},{"id":3,"name":"Deployment","w":24,"topics":["Preparing artifacts: .zip packages vs container images in ECR, Lambda layers, dependency packaging, CodeArtifact","AWS SAM: template structure, sam build, sam deploy --guided, sam local invoke / start-api, samconfig.toml","CloudFormation: templates, parameters, outputs and exports, Fn::ImportValue, change sets, packaging local artifacts to S3","AWS CDK basics: constructs, cdk bootstrap, cdk synth, cdk deploy","Lambda versions and aliases; weighted aliases; CodeDeploy canary, linear and all-at-once traffic shifting with alarm rollback","API Gateway stages, stage variables, deployments, mock integrations, canary releases","Elastic Beanstalk deployment policies: all at once, rolling, rolling with additional batch, immutable, traffic splitting, blue/green URL swap","CodePipeline stages and actions, manual approvals; CodeBuild buildspec phases and artifacts","CodeDeploy appspec.yml lifecycle hooks for EC2, Lambda and ECS; the CodeDeploy agent","Testing in development environments: unit tests in CI, integration tests against deployed stages, AppConfig feature flags and gradual configuration rollout"],"notes":["Task statements 3.1–3.4 (DVA-C02 exam guide)"],"labs":["Free Tier: install the SAM CLI and Docker, run sam init with the Hello World template, test with sam local invoke and sam local start-api, then deploy with sam deploy --guided and remove everything with sam delete.","Free Tier: publish two versions of a Lambda function, create a live alias with 90/10 weighted routing, and add DeploymentPreference: Canary10Percent5Minutes in the SAM template to watch CodeDeploy shift traffic. CodeDeploy for Lambda has no extra charge.","Mostly free: create a CodePipeline with a GitHub or CodeCommit-compatible source, a CodeBuild stage that runs unit tests from buildspec.yml, and a manual approval. Check pricing first: CodeBuild includes free build minutes each month and a V2 pipeline is billed per action execution minute, so delete the pipeline when done."]},{"id":4,"name":"Troubleshooting and Optimization","w":18,"topics":["Root cause analysis with CloudWatch Logs, Logs Insights queries, metrics and dashboards","Common Lambda errors: throttling (429), timeouts, AccessDenied from the execution role, malformed proxy responses (502), API Gateway 504 integration timeouts","AWS X-Ray: segments, subsegments, annotations vs metadata, sampling, active tracing, the X-Ray daemon/CloudWatch agent","Custom metrics: PutMetricData, CloudWatch embedded metric format, high-resolution metrics","CloudWatch alarms with SNS notifications; structured logging and correlation IDs","Lambda performance: memory/CPU tuning, cold starts, provisioned concurrency, reserved concurrency","Stream and queue troubleshooting: Kinesis IteratorAge, parallelization factor, SQS dead-letter queues and redrive","DynamoDB optimization: hot partitions, key design, on-demand vs provisioned capacity, adaptive capacity","Caching for performance: API Gateway stage caching, CloudFront, ElastiCache, DAX"],"notes":["Task statements 4.1–4.3 (DVA-C02 exam guide)"],"labs":["Free Tier: turn on active tracing for a Lambda function behind API Gateway, add an X-Ray subsegment with an annotation (for example tier=gold), and filter traces by it in the console. X-Ray includes a monthly free tier of traces.","Free Tier: make a function log JSON with a requestId, deliberately throw errors, then write a CloudWatch Logs Insights query that finds the slowest and failing requests; create an alarm on Errors that emails you through SNS.","Free Tier: run the open-source AWS Lambda Power Tuning tool (or invoke manually at 128, 512 and 1024 MB) against a CPU-heavy function and compare duration and cost. Avoid leaving provisioned concurrency or API Gateway caching enabled: both bill by the hour and are not in the Free Tier."]}]});
